cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Six Dialects"
version: 0.1.0
license: "Apache-2.0"
type: software
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
abstract: >-
  Six Dialects reads a software project through six moral grammars and reports,
  for each, the premise it starts from, what in the project would be read as
  wrong there, and one concrete design decision that follows. It then shows
  where two grammars contradict each other on that specific case, and names the
  point at which a person stops exercising their own judgment. The six are
  Brussels (rights), Washington (freedom), Beijing (group stability), London
  (catastrophic risk), Singapore (compliance) and Rome (dignity). Two commands:
  review reads a project, probe asks a model twelve questions with no right
  answer and reports which grammar it answers in. Six is a sample, not a census:
  the categories are defined by one person and there is no independent ground
  truth yet, so every output is a reading, not a measure. The conditions under
  which a category enters or is retired are stated in the repository.
authors:
  - given-names: Nicoletta
    family-names: Iacobacci
    affiliation: "Ethics of Example"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0274-4049"
keywords:
  - AI ethics
  - AI governance
  - responsible AI
  - value pluralism
  - human oversight
  - design review
  - large language models
  - moral reasoning
